            ('p', 'For most VAT-registered businesses, the VAT bill is not a surprise. It arrives on a predictable timetable and the amount builds up in plain sight, invoice by invoice. Yet it still catches plenty of owners out, usually because the money was sitting in the current account and quietly got spent. A little structure makes it far easier to pay on time without disrupting the rest of the business.'),
            ('h2', 'Know your dates'),
            ('p', 'For businesses on standard quarterly returns, the return and the payment are usually due one calendar month and seven days after the end of the VAT period. A quarter ending on 30 September, for example, is normally due by 7 November. The deadline doesn’t move for weekends or bank holidays, and the money needs to have reached HMRC by that date. If you pay by Direct Debit, HMRC collects a few working days after the deadline, which can help with timing.'),
            ('p', 'Returns are now submitted through Making Tax Digital compatible software, so your bookkeeping system should be able to show your running VAT position at any point in the quarter. Check the dates for your own stagger group on GOV.UK or with your accountant, as quarter ends vary between businesses.'),
            ('h2', 'Treat VAT as money you are holding, not money you have earned'),
            ('p', 'The simplest habit is also the most effective. When customers pay you, part of that payment belongs to HMRC. Many businesses move the VAT element into a separate savings or holding account every week or every month, so the balance is ready when the return is due.'),
