            ('p', 'Many directors are surprised to be asked for a personal guarantee. A limited company is a separate legal person, so it can feel as though its debts should stay with the company. A personal guarantee changes that for one specific debt. It is one of the most common requests in business lending, and also one of the most commonly signed without being fully understood.'),
            ('h2', 'What you are promising'),
            ('p', 'A personal guarantee is a promise by an individual, usually a director or shareholder, to repay the lender if the company can’t. If the business defaults, the lender can come to you personally for the amount covered by the guarantee. Depending on the wording, that may include interest, fees and the lender’s costs of recovery, not just the original loan.'),
            ('p', 'Lenders ask for guarantees because they show commitment and give them a second route to repayment. They are particularly common on unsecured business loans, where there is no specific asset to fall back on, but they can appear alongside secured lending too.'),
